  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Barbados Workers&#x2019; Union Labour College (Frank Walcott Labour College), established in 1974, has been constantly adapting its curriculum to the needs and trends of the world of work. With COVID-19 measures in place, the College launched an online platform in May / April  2021 to provide adapted the courses to online format. The online platform was launched on May 1, 2021 and since then the College has been offering training to members of the he Barbados Workers&#x2019; Union (BWU) members and workers in Barbados in general. This has been a very important service especially for workers who lost their jobs or are underemployed due to COVID-19.
This has also been an important tool for the union to stay connected with and support their members in the challenging times of COVID-19.
The online courses are now part of the College&#x2019;s curriculum; they start weekly and cover a wide range of topics (e.g. effective communication, work-life balance, negotiations, mental health, managing conflict, among others). In 2020, the ILO provided technical support to the BWU and Labour College to update the websites and create an online learning platform to digitalize and run online existing training courses. A series of virtual consultations were held with the collaboration of the International Training Centre of the ILO in Turin. 
In 2021 tutors of the Labour College successfully participated in the ILO and Turin Centre courses on digitalisation and e-learning.</narrative>
   </description>
